# A NEMA 23 STEPPER MOTOR.
#
# A real catalogue part rather than a shape invented to exercise a feature:
# every number below is a NEMA 23 dimension, so the result can be checked
# against a datasheet instead of against itself.
#
#   frame              56.4 mm square, corners cut back 4 mm
#   body length        76 mm
#   pilot boss         38.1 mm diameter, 1.6 mm proud of the face
#   shaft              6.35 mm diameter, 21 mm from the mounting face
#   mounting holes     5.1 mm on a 47.14 mm square, 12 mm deep
#
# It is built along +Z with the MOUNTING FACE ON TOP, at z = 76. That choice
# is not cosmetic: the boss, the shaft and the four tapped holes all belong to
# that one face, so putting it at a known height lets every later sketch name
# it by number instead of by picking.

echo --- the frame ---

# THE BODY IS AN OCTAGON, not a square: a stepper's corners are cut back so
# the four mounting bolts can pass the laminations. Eight clicks with the line
# tool, and the ninth returns to the first point -- the chain gives every
# corner a real Coincident constraint, so the profile closes because the
# geometry says so rather than because the numbers happen to agree.
#
# THE 4 mm CUT IS LOAD-BEARING, and it was wrong first. At 8 mm the corner
# chamfer passes 0.9 mm from each bolt-hole centre, so all four bores break
# out through it -- and the part still builds, still saves and still looks
# right from the front. What caught it was `measure`: the volume said only
# 2.87 holes' worth of material had gone. Arithmetic against a closed form is
# the only check that could have.
sketch Frame
tool line
click 24.2 28.2
click -24.2 28.2
click -28.2 24.2
click -28.2 -24.2
click -24.2 -28.2
click 24.2 -28.2
click 28.2 -24.2
click 28.2 24.2
click 24.2 28.2

# The laminated stack and both end caps in one prism. A real motor's caps are
# aluminium and its middle is steel, but they share this cross-section exactly
# -- and EP3D has no per-face material to tell them apart with, so modelling
# them as three solids would add two booleans and describe nothing extra.
pad Motor 76 as BodyLength

solve
echo --- the bare frame ---
measure

echo --- the front of the motor ---

# THE PILOT BOSS. What actually locates a stepper is this spigot, not the
# bolts: the bolt holes are clearance, and the boss is what the machine's
# mounting plate is bored for.
sketch Boss xy 76
tool circle
click 0 0
click 19.05 0
dimension diameter Circle1 38.1 as BossDiameter
pad Motor 1.6 as BossHeight
union Motor

# THE SHAFT, measured from the MOUNTING FACE like every datasheet quotes it --
# so it starts at z = 76 and runs up through the boss. The overlap is the
# union's problem, not the author's.
sketch Shaft xy 76
tool circle
click 0 0
click 3.175 0
dimension diameter Circle1 6.35 as ShaftDiameter
pad Motor 21 as ShaftLength
union Motor

solve
echo --- with the boss and the shaft ---
measure

echo --- the mounting holes ---

# THE BOLT PATTERN, said as CONSTRAINTS rather than as four typed coordinates.
#
# Four points that merely happen to sit on a square are four points that come
# apart the moment one is dragged. These four are held square by the geometry:
# the bottom pair share a v, the top pair share a v, the left pair share a u,
# the right pair share a u -- and then ONE dimension across and ONE up fixes
# the size of the whole pattern.
#
# `constrain horizontal` and `constrain vertical` take TWO POINTS here, which
# is the point-pair form: it says "these two are level" about points no line
# joins.
sketch Mounts xy 76
tool point
click -23.57 -23.57
click 23.57 -23.57
click 23.57 23.57
click -23.57 23.57

# One corner is pinned because nothing else in this sketch says WHERE the
# pattern sits -- only how big and how square it is. Centring it on the shaft
# axis would need construction geometry, which is a gap named honestly rather
# than papered over with two more typed numbers.
constrain fix Point1
constrain horizontal Point1 Point2
constrain horizontal Point4 Point3
constrain vertical Point1 Point4
constrain vertical Point2 Point3
dimension hdistance Point1 Point2 47.14 as BoltSpacing
dimension vdistance Point1 Point4 47.14 as BoltRise

# A NEGATIVE DEPTH DRILLS THE OTHER WAY along the sketch normal -- down into
# the end cap, not up into the air above it. These are the tapped blind holes
# a stepper is bolted through; a through hole here would run the length of the
# motor and out of the back, which is a different and much worse part.
hole Motor Mounts 5.1 -12

solve
echo --- the finished motor ---
measure

save build/stepper-motor.ep3d
